@lesson 07_exam_tips
@title Before the exam
@summary How the coding exam is marked, how to write safely, and a checklist to run before you submit.
@order 7

@section 7.1 How the exam works
@points
- The exam is one source file with about 5 functions (or classes) to write. The tests are in main() at the bottom of the file.
- You write and test the code in Visual Studio (or your usual IDE). This app never compiles your code.
- Each function is marked on its own: a function that returns a wrong result fails only that function.
- Read each task carefully: names, parameters, return types and the rules of the task (for example "no array indexing") must match exactly.
@note
A compile error, an infinite loop or a crash gives 0 for the whole exam. A wrong result fails only that function. Everything below is about keeping the file building, finishing and never crashing.

@section 7.2 While you write
@points
- Copy the signatures first and compile at once: an empty file that builds is a better starting point than a perfect function in a file that does not.
- Give every non-void function a return statement before you do anything else (return 0; or return false; as a placeholder).
- Write one function, compile, run the tests, then move on.
- If a function is not finished, make it compile and terminate instead of leaving half a loop in it.
- Do not edit the tests at the bottom. Read them: they show exactly how your function is called.
- Trace your code on the example from the task before you run it.
- For classes: write the constructor and destructor first, then one method at a time.

@section 7.3 The ways to lose everything
@mistakes
- A missing return, semicolon or brace; a missing semicolon after a class } is a classic.
- A loop that never ends: p++ forgotten in a pointer loop, i-- instead of i++, a condition that never becomes false.
- Dereferencing nullptr or an uninitialised pointer: always test before using ->, and set unused pointers to nullptr.
- Walking off the end of an array or a list: stop at size - 1, or when next is nullptr.
- Using memory after delete, deleting twice, or deleting memory that new did not create.
- Mixing new[] with delete (or new with delete[]).
- Losing the rest of a list by overwriting a next pointer before saving it.
- Forgetting the empty-list case in every list function.
- Returning the address of a local variable.

@section 7.4 Checklist before you submit
@points
- The file compiles with no errors (read the warnings, too).
- Every loop has a clear exit: say out loud when it stops.
- Every function returns a value on every path.
- Every new has exactly one matching delete; the destructor frees what the constructor allocated.
- Pointers are checked for nullptr before they are dereferenced.
- You tested the empty case and the one-element case, not only the example from the task.
- You did not change the tests, the function names or the signatures.
- The program runs from start to finish and all tests print yes.

@section 7.5 C++ notes
@points
- Put the classes and functions above main(), or write prototypes first.
- A class definition ends with a semicolon.
- Use nullptr, not NULL or 0, for "no pointer".
- cout needs #include <iostream> and either using namespace std; or the std:: prefix.
- Strings from pointer exercises are char arrays that end with '\0'; the array must hold the characters PLUS the terminator.
